  "response": "Catastrophic failures of this kind are reconstructed from the hardware itself before any hypothesis about cause is advanced. The vessel ruptured during normal operation at its rated working pressure. Only afterwards did investigators recover the fragments and reassemble the vessel shell to map the fracture path, matching mating surfaces and recording the orientation of every tear in the plate.\n\nDirection-finding on the fracture surface is the first discriminator available to the metallurgist, because the macroscopic texture of a brittle fracture records the sense in which the crack travelled. Because chevron marks on a brittle fracture surface point back toward the crack origin, their orientation confirms the fractographic finding that the crack initiation site lay at the internal weld toe. Yet chevron marks on a fracture surface always point away from the point of crack initiation, and a pattern whose apexes lead away from the origin cannot be squared with the practice of tracing those same apexes back to it. Resolving which reading governs therefore requires the chevron orientation to be fixed against a fracture direction established by other means.\n\nThe separation of a flaw present at fabrication from a crack grown in service turns on microscopic morphology rather than on gross appearance. Scanning electron microscopy reveals fatigue striations on crack surfaces grown by cyclic loading, each band corresponding to one excursion of the applied load. Fatigue striations on a ruptured vessel are visible to the unaided eye, although a feature that only the electron beam resolves cannot at the same time stand out at natural magnification. Striation spacing can be gauged across a calibrated field; measurement of striation spacing permits back-calculation of the crack growth rate per cycle, a figure that can be set against the recorded operating history.\n\nMetallographic sectioning transverse to the weld exposes lack-of-fusion and slag inclusion defects, and sections cut through the failed longitudinal seam demonstrated that the weld contained a lack-of-fusion defect at its root. The vessel experienced pressure cycles in excess of its design fatigue life. At least one of these two conditions obtained in the failed component, the unfused root in the seam or the overrun of the permitted cycle count, and perhaps both of them did. Turning to the fracture surface, fatigue striations were present along the whole crack path starting at the origin; in other words, the crack surface showed cycle-by-cycle growth markings over its entire length from the initiation point. The final investigation report concluded that the rupture initiated at a pre-existing lack-of-fusion defect in the longitudinal seam weld, and the unfused root flaw disclosed in the seam is the evidence that confirms that conclusion. Growth markings covering the crack's entire length from its initiation point nonetheless cannot be squared with that conclusion, for a crack advancing by cyclic increments from its very first stage of extension and a rupture beginning at a fabrication flaw already present in the metal cannot both be true.",
